The `xml_buf` is a stack buffer (`wchar_t xml_buf[N_XML_BUF+1]`), so the `pw[-1]` read in `GetSsmlAttribute` would be a stack-buffer-overflow, not heap. The `ssml_stack` is a static array, so writing past it would be a global-buffer-overflow.

The issue says "Heap-buffer-overflow READ 1". Let me look at the `VoiceFromStack` function more carefully. It reads `ssml_stack[0].voice_name` and `ssml_stack[0].language` with `strcpy`. But those are within the struct.

Let me look at the `ProcessSsmlTag` function more carefully. The `ssml_sp = &ssml_stack[*n_ssml_stack-1]` at line 677 - if `*n_ssml_stack` is 0, this reads before the array. But `n_ssml_stack` starts at 1 and is only decremented in `GetVoiceAttributes` when `n_ssml_stack > 1`. So it can't go below 1 through that path.

Wait, let me re-read the `GetVoiceAttributes` function. When processing a closing tag, it decrements `n_ssml_stack` locally (it's passed by value). But the caller passes `*n_ssml_stack` which is a pointer. Let me check how the return value is used... Actually, `n_ssml_stack` is passed by value to `GetVoiceAttributes`, so the decrement doesn't affect the caller's `*n_ssml_stack`. The caller's `*n_ssml_stack` is only modified in the `else` branch (opening tag) via `ssml_sp = &ssml_stack[n_ssml_stack++]` - but wait, that's also local since `n_ssml_stack` is passed by value!

Hmm, let me re-read. In `GetVoiceAttributes`:
```c
if (tag_type & SSML_CLOSE) {
    // delete a stack frame
    if (n_ssml_stack > 1)
        n_ssml_stack--;
} else {
    ...
    ssml_sp = &ssml_stack[n_ssml_stack++];
    ...
}
```

`n_ssml_stack` is a local copy (passed by value). So the caller's `*n_ssml_stack` is never updated by `GetVoiceAttributes`. The caller's `*n_ssml_stack` is only updated in `ProcessSsmlTag` via the `SSML_SPEAK + SSML_CLOSE` and `SSML_VOICE + SSML_CLOSE` cases which do `(*n_ssml_stack)--` in the while loops.

So the stack can grow unboundedly in `GetVoiceAttributes` since `n_ssml_stack` is local and never checked against `N_SSML_STACK`. Each opening `<voice>` or `<s xml:lang="...">` tag adds a frame. With 20+ nested tags, `ssml_stack[n_ssml_stack++]` writes past the end of the static array.

